{
  "about": "Verdicts both twins of the param schema must reproduce exactly: arsenal/web/lib/params.js (tests/params_schema.test.mjs) and arsenal/params.py (tests/test_params_schema.py). Descriptor sets and module headers are in descriptors.json. Expectations were derived by hand from the rules in the twins' headers (snap to the step grid from min with round-half-up, else 6 decimals; clamp; defaults for missing or null; derived params computed, never taken), not generated by either twin. A validate case's expected values are the set's defaults below with the case's patch applied. JSON has no NaN or Infinity, so the strings __NaN__, __Inf__ and __-Inf__ stand for them in inputs and decode outputs; both tests swap them in. Problems compare as [id, code] pairs in order: descriptor order first, then unknown keys sorted (by UTF-16 code units, as JavaScript sorts). Some inputs are deliberately hostile: a 401-digit integer (json.loads makes an int of it, JSON.parse makes Infinity, and both twins must call it a bad number), a leading byte-order mark in a URL, 45 emoji in a message (cut at 40 code points), derived expressions past the 128-token cap, range ends one apart past 2^53 (one number to JavaScript) and ranges without a step whose ends need 7 and 10 decimals (a range without a step keeps 6 decimals or as many as its ends need, up to 9, and rounding never leaves the range). module_checks run paramsFromModule then checkDescriptors; every module_bad_headers header must make paramsFromModule throw.",
